UID:35768-1796731200-1796742000@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Logic and Philosophy of Science Group (Gordon Belot\, Michigan)
DESCRIPTION:The Logic and Philosophy of Science Research Group is pleased to welcome as its guest speaker Gordon Belot\, the Lawrence Sklar Collegiate Professor of Philosophy at the University of Michigan. Professor Belot’s primary interests are in philosophy of physics\, philosophy of science\, metaphysics\, and epistemology. His most recent book\, Accelerating Expansion: Philosophy and Physics with a Positive Cosmological Constant\, explores some of the philosophical consequences of modern cosmology\, focusing on the significance of the discovery of the accelerating expansion of the universe for our understanding of time\, geometry\, and physics. \nTalk Title\nComputable Epistemology—An Idiosyncratic Primer \nTalk Abstract\nSome morals. (a) In some settings\, there is no such thing as an optimal method for learning (so someone who tells you there is a unique rational response to any body of evidence may be telling you something strange). (b) In some settings\, induction-unfriendly scenarios are common (and do not require demonic fine-tuning). (c) In some settings\, computable learners pay a price for following intuitively reasonable strategies (like relying on the simplest hypothesis consistent with their data). (d) In some settings\, computable learners pay no price for being Bayesian—but only if they are willing to use priors inconsistent with their background knowledge.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/logic-and-philosophy-of-science-group-gordon-belot-michigan/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Belot-new-resized.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:UofT Philosophy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261210T150000
