LAST-MODIFIED:20261106T215351Z
UID:35843-1796914800-1796922000@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Aesthetics Reading Group (Alexandra King\, Simon Fraser University)
DESCRIPTION:The Aesthetics Reading Group is pleased to welcome as guest speaker Alexandra King\, associate professor in the Department of Philosophy at Simon Fraser University. Currently\, she is thinking through what aesthetic normativity looks like: the shape it takes\, its parallels and interactions with other varieties of normativity (like the moral and epistemic)\, and whether it can be said to exist at all. Within aesthetics\, she is also interested in an assortment of issues\, including subtlety\, high and low art\, aesthetic/artistic agency\, and applied arts. She also owns\, runs\, and regularly writes for the aesthetics and philosophy of art blog Aesthetics for Birds\, which aims to make concepts and arguments in academic aesthetics accessible to a wide\, public audience. \nTalk Title\nWhat Is the Aesthetic? \nTalk Abstract\nTalk of aesthetics – including among other things talk of aesthetic reasons and value\, aesthetic practices\, and aesthetic agency – is on the rise recently. These discussions aim to acknowledge the ways aesthetics can inform such debates and be informed by them. But it often remains unclear what\, exactly\, makes a value or reason or practice aesthetic\, especially once we distinguish the aesthetic from the artistic. To sidestep this difficulty\, a recent trend in aesthetics is to explicitly set aside the talk’s titular question\, claiming that such a question needn’t be answered before proceeding. This talk challenges that tendency\, arguing that we need a more precise conception of the subject matter at hand if we are to make real progress in giving such accounts. I propose and defend a view on which the aesthetic has to do with sensation\, not narrowly restricted to sense perception\, but construed more broadly to include mental imagery as well as feeling and emotion\, ideation\, comprehension\, and understanding. I then examine which conclusions this framework licenses us to draw about aesthetic reasons and value\, practices\, and agency.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/aesthetics-reading-group-alexandra-king-simon-fraser-university/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/alex-king.png
END:VEVENT
