UID:35773-1797001200-1797008400@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:Early Modern Philosophy Research Interest Group (Andreea Mihali\, Wilfrid Laurier)
DESCRIPTION:The Early Modern Philosophy Research Interest Group is pleased to welcome as its guest speaker Andreea Mihali (Wilfrid Laurier University) who is an expert in critical thinking\, informal logic and argumentation theory\, modern philosophy (Descartes to Kant) and visual and musical arguments. \nTalk Title\nDescartes’ Omissions \nTalk Abstract\nIn the Meditations Descartes insists that readers closely follow the example and meditating steps set out in this text (AT VII\, 10; CSM II\, 8). Given this emphasis on the right order of reasons\, it is at least prima facie surprising to also find the following remarks in the Second Replies: “Moreover there are many truths which although it is vital to be aware of them – this method [analysis] often scarcely mentions since they are transparently clear to anyone who gives them his attention” (AT VII\, 156; CSM II\, 110 – emphasis added).  \nA careful reading of Descartes’ works shows that such remarks regarding things left out of Descartes’ actual text are far from being unique. This paper aims to classify Descartes’ omissions\, to inquire into what these omissions tell us about Descartes’ approach and to make some preliminary remarks about the light these omissions shed on his explicit statements and theses. \nAfter discussing and backing up with textual evidence seven criteria of classification of omissions (content-relatedness\, scope\, means of identification\, deliberateness\, motivation\, purpose and who is expected to remedy these omissions)\, I will apply them to Descartes’ published works. This application will show that Descartes’ account of the progression of his published works in the Preface to the French Principles (progression from the Discourse and the three accompanying Essays\, through the Meditations\, to the Principles – Latin and French)\, this progression in effect also tracks omissions and corresponding additions.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/early-modern-philosophy-research-interest-group-andreea-mihali-wilfrid-laurier/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Philosophy-Circular-logo-225.png
ORGANIZER;CN="Tarek Dika":MAILTO:tarek.dika@utoronto.ca
END:VEVENT
